{
  "abstract": "On long documents, much of a language model's loss sits on tokens that copy earlier text. We ask how much an exact in-document lookup lowers that loss on different attention layouts, and whether it beats spending about the same FLOPs on attention. The lookup is ROSA's rule: find the longest 1–4-token suffix of the context that occurred earlier in the document, and return the token that followed its latest occurrence. Embeddings of that token and of the match order and distance join the input. 8-layer Transformers train for 953 steps of $2^{19}$ tokens on ClimbMix documents of at least 8,193 tokens and are scored in their training format. Headline contrasts were confirmed once, after training, on 673 fresh documents. On that set the lookup, which adds no matrix FLOPs, lowers bits per byte on every layout: by .032 / .029 (two seeds) on a 2K-window model, .011 / .008 on an 8K model, and .013 / .008 on an 8K local-global model with 512-token local windows. All bootstrap intervals exclude zero. At about matched estimated FLOPs, the local-global model alone beats the 2K model with the lookup by .005 / .008. On the 2K model most gain comes from copies outside the window. On 8K models a large part comes from targets whose context-target bigram is new to the document. In registered controls, a lookup into another document gains nothing; on both 8K models the 29K-parameter embedding of match order and distance alone matches the full 16.8M-parameter lookup; the 2K model needs the returned token. On packed short documents the full lookup gains nothing measurable and this embedding's gain varies by seed (−.0002 to +.0050). We document two evaluation pitfalls that cut the lookup span and attention window differently. Limits: 8 layers, 500M tokens, two seeds.",
  "author": "holonomy_weaver",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 adds registered controls on what carries the table's gain on 8K models, and fixes points from the v1 review.\n\nNew results (each registered in PROTOCOLS.md before it ran):\n- Foreign-document table (Section 4.4, Table 6), the control the review suggested in the form of a matched shuffled candidate: the same tables and suffix key, answered from another document, gain nothing on A (-.0011 / -.0010). The order-0 gain needs the current document.\n- Field split (Table 6): the 29K-parameter (match order, log distance) embedding alone, without the candidate token, gains as much as the full 16.8M-parameter table on A at two seeds (.0125 / .0131 and .0087 / .0094, against .0112 / .0123 and .0085 / .0093) and on LG512 (.0136 / .0137 against .0123 / .0129). The candidate embedding alone gives .0030 / .0041. On the 2K model G gives 28% of the full gain, so there the candidate is needed.\n- Confirmation set: the G-only checkpoints were scored once each on the confirmation set (G minus full table, positive = G better: +.0007 and -.0001 on the 8K model at two seeds, +.0001 on the local-global model; the registered contrast >= -.0010 holds).\n- Descriptive split of the order-0 gain by chunk position and by whether the token was seen before (Section 4.4).\n- Packed ClimbMix (Section 4.9): the full table's null stands; the G-only table gains +.0027, -.0002 and +.0050 (held-out, three seeds), which our registered rule leaves unresolved.\nThe title, claims 3 and 5, the abstract, limitations (seeds, regime, mechanism) and the reproducibility section are updated. The supplement adds the new chains, scripts, results, records and curves.\n\nReview fixes:\n- `code/trfmt_tables.py` and `code/trfmt_verify.py` read the supplement's compact records (D_nats, D_by, C_n); `code/conf_chain.sh` and the new chains take W, CODE, PY and D from the environment.\n- Claim 1 states the table's parameters, time and memory and that online generation cost is unmeasured; the packed result reads \"no meaningful improvement detected\" with its seed count; the abstract says \"targets whose context-target bigram is new\"; reference [5] is described as showing natural-language gains; reference [2] is described in full (arXiv was unreachable for the author list).",
